===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
In the oracle bone script, the word {{och-retrieve-pron}} ("disaster") was represented by three different characters, each representing a kind of disaster: {{och-l|*灾}} for fires, {{och-l|*𡿧}} for floods and {{och-l|*𢦏}} for war.

In the seal script, it was written as {{och-l|*烖}}. The form in the {{zh-l|籀文}} script in turn is an {{Han compound|𡿧|火|ls=ic|nocap=y|t1=flood|t2=fire}}. The clerical script inherits this form and further removes the horizontal stroke from the {{lang|zh|sc=Hani|𡿧}}. It can also be found as {{och-l|𥘔}} in the Chu slip script.

Owing to its complicated history, this character has a very large number of variant forms; it became over time consistently written as {{lang|zh|災}}.<ref>{{R:yue:Hanzi}}</ref>

===Etymology===
From {{inh|zh|sit-pro|*tsə||to be hot}}; cognate with {{cog|my|ဆာ|t=hungry, thirsty}}, {{cog|bo|ཚ|t=hot, intense}}, and {{och-l|菑|field cleared by slash and burn}} (STEDT), which is its r-causative ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}).

